    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d690">

                <pb facs="0543" n="535" sameAs="frus:frus1937v04/pg535"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94111/60: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-09-18T20:00:00+08:00">September 18, 1937—8
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received September 18—1:22 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>683. Department’s 239, September 15, 8 p.m. During a visit with the Minister for
                    Foreign Affairs<anchor n="25" xml:id="d690-f1"/> this morning I introduced the
                    subject of President’s order and explained situation along the line of <pb
                        facs="0544" n="536" xml:id="pg536"/> views expressed by the Department.
                    Doctor Wang said that Chinese Government was deeply disappointed at interruption
                    of shipment of materials. He stated that in the light of the mandatory character
                    of the Neutrality Act he appreciated attitude and moderate action taken.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d690-f1" n="25" xml:id="d690-f1-content">
                <p>Wang Chung-hui.</p>
